 "issue": "openagents#9381",
 "annotator": "A Devin session (an automated coding agent), not a person. The first annotator wrote every stored label; this file records one independent second reading. It says whether the labels are reproducible by a careful reader who did not write them, and nothing about whether they are correct.",
 "procedure": [
  "Sample 100 item ids from the calibration and development partitions, stratified by family in the suite's proportions (routing 51, urgency 31, severity 18), with Python's `random.Random(9381)`, then shuffle. The locked partition was not read.",
  "Write each item's state, question instructions, and criteria to a file with the `truth` field removed, and label every item from that file alone.",
  "Compare against the stored labels only after all 100 labels were written. No label was changed after the comparison.",
  "Cohen's kappa per family, over the labels each family uses. `agreement_wilson_95` is a Wilson score interval for the observed agreement, so the ceiling's own uncertainty is beside it: 18 severity items agreeing on all 18 does not establish agreement near 1.0."
 ],
 "reading": "Observed agreement is the ceiling a perfect door could reach on that family if the second reader's labels were the truth. Put it beside every accuracy figure for the family. It is a sample estimate from 100 items and one second reader; the intervals say how loose it is.",
